portunity and  said well we're going to do something  that complements what's already there  and then build something a little bit  bigger  and the result is our virtual  comprehensive services so this for those  familiar with a stepped care model is  somewhat similar to that and it begins  at the foundation which is a 24-hour  navigation hub and crisis support call  line  this call line and navigation hub is  web-based i'll show you the website in a  moment  what this does is it provides us with  the opportunity  to have a place for people to call if  they're concerned about drugs or alcohol  either for themselves or their family  the the line is staffed by those peer  recovery support specialists that i  mentioned to you earlier that the  backbone of what we do is having peers  involved and they're able both to talk  to somebody who's wondering if they're  having an issue  or perhaps has recently relapsed but  then they're also there to say hey are  you interested in treatment  one of the biggest barriers is again  finding treatment and so these  individuals are able to say well we can  connect you to our program  and they can receive  as close to on-demand as possible um  treatment  offerings directly from that so if you  imagine it it's a call  connected to triage connected to care  connected to visits and because it's  virtual an individual does not even need  to leave their home or the place where  they're calling from  this includes medication services as  well as the initiation of buprenorphine  for as long as we're allowed to do that  we're currently able to do that because  of some of the loosening of the  regulations around buprenorphine um so  we will start somebody um  on treatment for an opioid use disorder  if it's appropriate after thorough  evaluation  but low barrier we're able to do that  over the phone last week we had an  example where an individual called  um they  were appropriate for treatment with  buprenorphine for an opioid use disorder  but the physician at our clinic the  bewell texas clinic  felt like it probably needed another  person's eyes on them before they were  willing to do that so this individual  actually went to their primary care  doctor and said hey i have an addiction  medicine doc who wants to start me in  treatment can can i do that from your  office so that we're sure that it's safe  um and so it was a really great  partnership where someone that we'd  never met before um until that visit was  partnered with their primary care doctor  who again we'd never met before but  through consultation and liaison in a  video visit we were able to start that  person on life-saving treatment using  buprenorphine and again as a reminder  for those of you who may not know  the rate that this the  treatment initiation on buprenorphine  has an incredible impact um in reducing  morbidity and mortality associated with  opioid use disorder so it's a nice  example  in practice of what we're trying to  accomplish with this goal so that's that  treatment and care coordination piece  and then because of the pandemic and the  strain  that  that dealing with the pandemic has  placed on health care workers and the  stress that they've experienced we are  seeing an alarming increase in the use  of substances and mental health issues  among health care workers so we have a  particular carve out for specialized  peers  and services and treatment available for  health care workers as part of this  program as well  so this is the website for recovery  texas um we uh  we have this program um we're currently  staffed this is all very new we  currently start from 9 a.m to 9 00 p.m  and we're going 24 hours within the next  30 days and this is a place where people  are able to get help immediately and  have someone i'm speaking to them  chatting or  being able to communicate with them in  this in in real time  and then that  last  excuse me so this and then the third leg  in our third legged stool is that  telementoring so we are standing up a  telementoring institute to encourage  primary care providers  um  other specialties um to become involved  in treating substance use disorder um  and reducing the morbidity and mortality  and ending the overdose crisis or the  opioid crisis which is the name of this  meeting um by providing  appropriate mentoring and support and  specialty care consultation um for uh  those who who would be willing to treat  substance use disorder so this again is  a no-cost telementoring institute to  support improving treatment outcomes we  offer a practice-based culturally  responsive approaches to care  we  and the intent is to advance the quality  of care and the skills and competency of  healthcare workers broadly defined so  physicians to emf first responders  nursing staff medical technicians  behavioral health workforce to increase  access to substance use treatment so  that when these individuals come into  contact with someone with a substance  use disorder and opioid use disorder  they're able to respond with the very  best care and know that they've got  referral systems in place and the  complication liaison with experts and  opioid use disorders so these are  um a variety of activities that we do um  the the anchor platform for that is a  program that was developed out of the  university of new mexico called project  echo project  echo is a video based  mentoring consultation leads liaison  support service  it's hipaa compliant and it provides us  the opportunity to engage individuals so  in our programs we have a buprenorphine  community of practice where individuals  can join our mentoring program  there are twice monthly  community practice sessions where  didactics about getting started with  buprenorphine treatment or complex cases  for example we've had  conversations around treating hepatitis  c  in the context of opioid use disorder or  benzodiazepines and the co-use of  benzodiazepines and buprenorphine  boarded addiction medicine experts that  work with us are on these calls  and then the bulk of the conversation is  actually where someone who's part of our  community of practice a program  presents a case and that case is  discussed  and  feedback is given on that and then  question and answer so it's different  than just a didactic it's really about  that conversation around patients  it also helps to extend  supervision  of the work that people are doing and  provide  programs that would not otherwise have  access to this type of mentoring  outlying areas rural communities or  underserved communities with low access  this really is a way to do that  we also have a major get waiver campaign  for buprenorphine and then finally we  have our texas substance use symposium  which is dedicated to interprofessional  learning around improving access to high  quality evidence-based care for  substance use disorder  so one of the reasons uh that i got into  this work uh and working and partnering  with the state and really looking at  transformative change using science  is because at some point i just was  overwhelmed by the number of pictures of  parents holding pictures of their dead  children  these are all people including the woman  at the center from san antonio that i  met several years ago at international  overdose awareness day in august  in san antonio who lost her son  to  opioids and opioid poisoning  we have to do something differently we  have to keep centering conversations in  our country around substance use and the  incredible impact that it is having on  families and the quality of life of  people in all communities  particularly communities  of color  particularly in communities that are  rural and particularly in communities  that don't have the same access and  equitable care and quality of care  that others of us do in the united  states  so with that i will stop and i think i  am on time and we can maybe take a few  questions  thank you dr potter for that informative  presentation  and we will now start the live q a  portion of this webinar and if you have  any questions you want to ask please do  so now just click on that ask a question  box located on the far  left of your screen  so let's get started we already have  some great questions coming in  our first question  does  your use of the term evidence-based mean  that there are randomized control trials  yeah that's actually a really great  question um and so i'm so glad that it  came up i debated whether to put what i  mean by that so  evidence-based care  there are definitions of that it means  that yes you do use the best possible  evidence available  it's also informed by the clinician's  experience and the patient's experience  and their desire  and their desire and their outcomes so  it's patient-centric  and it's informed by  the science but it does have that piece  of the clinician as part of the  decision-making process unfortunately i  wish we had evidence-based practices or  evidence-based treatments for everything  to do with substance use and  unfortunately we just don't have that um  and you know any randomized controlled  trial  when you take it into the the one-to-one  experience between a patient  and a provider  are  is unique and so you need to be informed  by the science but you can't just let  randomized controlled trials rule  entirely there's also the importance of  practice based evidence what we learn  from clinical experience can be  extremely valuable um the challenge is  it's got to be informed by the  randomized controlled trials  thank you so much dr potter our next  question coming in does texas have a  mandatory treatment for s-u-d-o-u-d in  the prison system  hmm  so  not to my knowledge actually it's more  the case that there's a greater demand  in texas for treatment in the criminal  justice system then there is capacity to  serve it there is a program funded by  health and human services texas targeted  opioid response that is working in some  of the specialized prison systems to  expand access to medication one of the  things that underlies that question  that's unfortunate is very often when  someone enters the criminal justice  system they no longer have access to  their treatment um so somebody might be  taking methadone um they may be  incarcerated for a period of time and  then they don't have methadone anymore  and it actually creates a very dangerous  situation when that person is is  released  because they do not have  their particular risk for overdose and  unintentional opioid poisoning at that  time  because they no longer have the  physiological tolerance to higher levels  of opioids and unfortunately if they do  make a decision to use an opioid after  release they'll be at greater risk of  death um so no there is no mandate  um  so i think i think i answered that one  i was going to ramble about something  thank you so much and thanks to our  audience for these great questions would  you talk a little bit more about the  stigma impact substance use disorder  treatment  yeah so  you know stigma is a tough thing i can't  um emphasize enough  the  the impact  of historical stigma on substance use  disorders one of the most um  strange to use the word refreshing but  one of the most refreshing things that  i've seen actually since about 2016  was that we are increasingly having more  conversations around substance use um in  unexpected places um  uh  the the i i do not follow british  royalty but um i think it's prince  william's wife kate kate middleton  that's it kate middleton um just  recently put out a statement um and has  taken on talking about addiction as  something on on her platform that would  never have happened 15 years ago  in the 2016 election on both sides we  were having conversations um around  substance use uh and and impacting some  of the candidates lives and their  families um and i was watching college  game day a few weeks ago and ashton  kutcher was on there talking around  talking about alcohol and that it can  cause problems for some even though it's  it's he likes to drink so  it's  there's some hope that stigma  is starting to reduce  i don't necessarily take on stigma by  talking about stigma what we do in our  work is we just do things differently um  and that sounds glib but there's some  simple things that we can do um  in the way we treat people that will  destigmatize just naturally that's why  i said welcome  we're be well texas and we're so glad  you're here  because wherever people are we want to  meet them where they're at and we want  to treat them with respect and dignity  but stigma  affects us in three ways there's  societal stigma which we've talked a  little bit about  there's uh stigma  from self and then there's other people  stigma that they press upon you  and so we've even seen physicians who  want to treat opioid use disorder who do  not treat because they view the other  physicians in their practices would  judge them negatively for their  willingness to treat substance use  disorder so it has a huge impact um and  we just have to do things differently  thank you so much we have time for two  more questions would you comment on the  impact  of what we are seeing with opioid use  disorders and overdose on people with  chronic pain  yeah  so one of the unfortunate uh  things that has happened with our  increased concern around opioid use  disorder is that there's been um  supply-side solutions and so when i say  supply-side solutions i'm talking about  access to opioid analgesics so these are  those medications that can be diverted  but are actually prescribed to  individuals with chronic pain um in  response to their very real pain  many individuals with chronic pain have  seen a dramatic i've seen a cessation or  a dramatic reduction  in their access to opioids  it is true that the evidence for opioids  to treat chronic pain is mixed  but  the way that we have handled this in our  country using these supply-side  solutions really has had some fairly  dramatic unintended consequences that  have adversely impacted people with  chronic pain um they've been considered  speaking of stigma they've been treated  as if they have opioid use disorders  when they had no symptom of an opioid  use disorder they've been questioned uh  about their opioid use they've been  judged and they've lost access  much more rapidly than probably would be  appropriate  uh we are seeing some of the  consequences of that so as much as we  talk about uh how we do we address  opioid use disorder what we're not  talking as much about and i think this  is changing a little bit is how this has  impacted people with chronic pain there  are studies going on right now because  of heightened concern around suicide  an  escalation of suicide in individuals  with chronic pain  and one of the speculations is that may  be a result of the dramatic and rapid  cessation of access to opioids which  they had been using to treat their pain  thank you so much we have time for one  more question  as we accept this pandemic what do you  foresee will be the impact on substance  use and mental health  so as i alluded to  we are seeing some warning signs that  there has been a fairly significant  uptick in mental health and substance  use  problematic substance use  in the wake of the pandemic  some have gone so far as to say the next  wave of the pandemic is to uh is is the  mental health curve so we will see this  rapid escalation so some there's been  some coining of terms like flattening  the mental health and substance use  disorder curve  you know  people are extraordinarily resilient  um and uh  most people even with an increase in  substance use and even with  intermittent uh  issue with a mental health like an  escalation anxiety or depression or what  have you  most people will be okay  and that's a good thing  on the other hand  every life is valuable and precious and  we have to have the infrastructure and  we have to have the capacity  um to be able to serve all individuals  who are struggling with a mental health  or a substance use disorder  the lack of equity in care uh is just a  terrible issue the lack of parity um  that we are still seeing um in terms of  of people's ability to get mental health  and substance use disorder treatment  relative to physical health issues um is  significant uh and i i'm assuming that  the folks who took the time to join  today are at least curious about how to  mitigate that um but but i think it's  going to be  a while i we are concerned and we need  to be ready for the problems that are  coming because the the downstream  consequences could be additional deaths  in the wrong direction  absolutely dr potter thank you so much  for your presentation today would you  like to provide any closing remarks to  our audience before we go  so i i guess i just say thank you so  much um i do hope that people will think  of this not as an opioid crisis um that  they'll think about about this as um  a substance use crisis and also  work to better understand their own  personal relationships with substances  including alcohol in their lives and  approach this from a state of normal  behavior  and understand when normal  becomes problematic  we need resources and backstops for that  um  and try to do something differently uh  this this week or this month when you  run into someone um rather than judge  about substances and substance use maybe  think about a little bit different way  to approach it um in our own lives as  well within our professional life  it's wonderful well thank you so much  and again thank you for your important  research as well before we go i want to  thank our audience for joining us today  and for our interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration and this  webcast can be viewed on demand for two  years until october 2023 laborats will  alert you via email when it's available  for replay we encourage you to share  that email with your colleagues who may  have missed today's live event until  next time take care everyone stay safe  bye  you

